    summary: "Galleri is GRAIL's multi-cancer early detection test: targeted methylation sequencing of cell-free DNA, read by a machine-learned classifier that reports whether a cancer signal is present and, if so, its predicted cancer signal origin. GRAIL markets it for more than 50 cancer types. It has been sold in the United States from GRAIL's CLIA-certified laboratory since 2021; GRAIL's cost page states it has not been cleared or approved by the FDA and gives a list price of $949. GRAIL submitted its premarket approval application on 29 January 2026, and on 23 September 2026 the FDA's Molecular and Clinical Genetics Devices Panel voted seven to two, with one abstention, that its benefits outweigh its risks for adults aged 50 and older (safety unanimous; effectiveness six to four), according to GRAIL. No FDA decision had been announced by 29 October 2026. Medicare coverage of FDA-authorised multi-cancer tests was enacted in February 2026 (Public Law 119-75, section 6221) for tests furnished from 1 January 2029.\n\nThe evidence. In the third CCGA case-control substudy (Klein 2021) specificity was 99.5% and sensitivity 16.8% at stage I, 40.4% at stage II, 77.0% at stage III and 90.1% at stage IV. In PATHFINDER 2 (Nature Medicine 2026; 35,878 enrolled, 32,007 analysable), single-arm, positive predictive value was 60.3%, specificity 99.64%, episode sensitivity 39.3% for all cancers and 69.8% for 12 prespecified cancers, and 185 people were screened to find one cancer. In the randomised NHS-Galleri trial (142,250 people aged 50 to 77 in England), the test-performance paper (Nature Medicine 2026) reports positive predictive values of 58.0, 50.4 and 45.8% across three rounds and episode sensitivity of 26.7 to 37.2% for all cancers, and the primary-endpoint paper (Sasieni, NEJM 2026) reports that the incidence of stage III or IV cancer did not differ between the groups (incidence rate ratio 1.03; 95% CI 0.92 to 1.14), with a stage IV incidence rate ratio of 0.86 (0.74 to 1.00) as a key secondary endpoint. Cancer deaths have not yet been reported. In people referred with symptoms (SYMPLIFY, Lancet Oncology 2023) it detected 24.2% of stage I cancers.",
