    summary: "Shield is Guardant Health's blood-based colorectal cancer screening test, which reads cell-free DNA in blood for signs of a tumour. The FDA approved it on 26 July 2024 (PMA P230009) for colorectal cancer screening in people at average risk aged 45 and over; Guardant describes it as the first blood test approved as a primary screening option. An earlier blood test, Epi proColon (FDA decision of 12 April 2016), was indicated only for adults who had been offered other screening and had not completed it. Medicare's coverage rule (NCD 210.3) covers a qualifying test once every three years for people aged 45 to 85 at average risk, and Guardant says Shield meets its performance requirements. In the ECLIPSE study it detected 83% of colorectal cancers with 90% specificity for advanced neoplasia, but only 13% of advanced precancerous lesions, which colonoscopy removes to prevent cancer. Its value therefore lies in reaching people who decline colonoscopy or stool tests, and any positive result must be followed by colonoscopy. The approved test first combined methylation, fragment and mutation signals; on 22 July 2026 the FDA approved a change to a methylation-only workflow. In June 2025 Guardant announced FDA Breakthrough Device designation for Shield MCD, a multi-cancer results report that Guardant's own site says is not part of the FDA-approved test. For a newcomer: a convenient blood test for bowel cancer that catches cancers well but polyps poorly.",
